A||||5||sailordude||Aftermarket A/C||||1||
Z||000000||sailordude||06-30-2001||10:55 AM||||Does anyone know how difficult it would be to add A/C to my car.  Has anyone done this before? How much did it cost? Did you do it yourself or have it done?<P>Thanks again guys,<P>Darren||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||06-30-2001||12:30 PM||||Now that would be labor and $ intensive.  If it were me I would look for a fiero with a/c to buy and drive or swap into.  If you don't have power windows, look into that cause it is not unbearable with the windows down I find.  BUT A/C IS NICE.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||06-30-2001||01:08 PM||||It would be extremely dificult to add after market. If you can find a system that will fit.<P>Migrate factory air... not fun either.<P>You'll have to rip apart the entire dash to remove/install the interior stuff. There are probably a bunch of wire harness items to move as well. All this on top of moving the AC compressor, under car lines, etc... You'll also have to pull the radiator to gat the condenser in. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||sailordude||06-30-2001||03:05 PM||||THanks for the info guys.  It sounds like I'll just stick with the heat.  I definatly do not have the $$$ to spend on A/C at the moment.||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadaver||07-04-2001||02:41 AM||||You guys defination of dificult is way different than mine. I have installed factory air in my truck and in a mustang. It did take some time but it wasent hard. Doing it in a fiero would be a little more complex but it still not hard to do.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||07-04-2001||12:21 PM||||Keep in mind...<P>The Fiero radiator has to come out... this can cause it to develope leaks unless it's done very carfully. (I hate plastic tanks...)<P>The entire dash and heating system has to come out.<P>There are a bunch of wire changes. It also requires connection to terminals on the ECM so that the ECM can boost idle when the comressor is on.<P>You have to fight with AC lines that run the lenght of the car.<P>It's not imposible but I would not recomend it for most people to try. Plan on the job taking at least 3-4 days. The dash work alone will take an entire day or more. Especially if you've never done it before. I spent a big chunk of a day just installing my power window harness into the proper location.||||reg||1
